1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,060
OK, are you all settled? Well, first of all, welcome to Cardiff University.

2
00:00:06,060 --> 00:00:09,160
I'm here to explain what we can offer you.

3
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,846
Now, as a new student at the university,

4
00:00:11,846 --> 00:00:18,560
you will probably need some sort of guidance to help you to use the library effectively to study and research.

5
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:24,200
Some of you have asked about a guided tour but we find this rather muddles people.

6
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:25,791
So, in this first week,

7
00:00:25,791 --> 00:00:31,200
we run a series of talks which focus on different aspects of the library and its resources.

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:37,791
You'll also find that to get the most out of the library you really do need to be computer literate

9
00:00:37,791 --> 00:00:40,757
and so all this term we run small classes

10
00:00:40,757 --> 00:00:45,370
which will bring you up to speed on how to access the computer-loaded information.

11
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:50,000
OK, now let me give you an outline of what's available to you.

12
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:54,360
You'll find that the computers are increasingly used as a research tool.

13
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:57,640
Many students do most of their research on the internet

14
00:00:57,940 --> 00:01:00,670
and the library computers are permanently online.

15
00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:02,146
Having found what you need,

16
00:01:02,146 --> 00:01:07,460
you'll find you can readily save texts on your personal computer space to print off when you need.

17
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:11,060
You might think that it is the fastest way to get information

18
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,940
but the links can be slow.

19
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:15,637
Clearly you can find lots on there

20
00:01:15,637 --> 00:01:21,800
but much of it is useless information as it is from highly debatable sources, so be critical.

21
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:30,440
You'll also find that the library has loaded several CD-ROMS onto the computers from specialist reference sources such as the MLA.

22
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:34,836
It means we can expand what we offer you at very little extra cost

23
00:01:34,836 --> 00:01:38,290
and saves us having to invest in more and more books.

24
00:01:38,290 --> 00:01:44,200
The CD-ROMS contain exactly the same information as the reference books as the two are updated together.

25
00:01:44,200 --> 00:01:48,703
Now most of you will need to refer to journal articles in your work

26
00:01:48,703 --> 00:01:53,850
and you'll find you can also access these online and we encourage you to do so.

27
00:01:53,850 --> 00:01:59,351
Clearly some of you will find the printed version more accessible as it sits on the shelves

28
00:01:59,351 --> 00:02:02,910
but I'm afraid the intention is to phase these out eventually.

29
00:02:02,910 --> 00:02:09,310
However, you will still be able to print off a version of the text rather than photocopying the journal pages.

30
00:02:09,310 --> 00:02:12,360
So you must get used to working online.

31
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:16,376
Naturally we do still have the full range of classic reference books,

32
00:02:16,376 --> 00:02:21,730
additional to the CD-ROMS, for you to use and there are several copies of each one.

33
00:02:21,730 --> 00:02:27,070
This is because some of you may prefer to borrow a book rather than sit in the library.

34
00:02:27,070 --> 00:02:29,760
There is a restricted loan time on these

35
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:32,730
so that they are not missing from the shelves for too long.

36
00:02:32,730 --> 00:02:36,820
Although there is a Section Manager for each part of the library

37
00:02:36,920 --> 00:02:38,768
they are very busy and so,

38
00:02:38,768 --> 00:02:41,140
if you do get stuck looking for things

39
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:43,800
you should ask the relevant Cataloguing Assistant.

40
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:48,020
As your Training Supervisor, I just oversee your induction

41
00:02:48,020 --> 00:02:52,900
and will not be around after this initial week.

42
00:02:52,900 --> 00:03:02,000
Some of you may be interested to know that the library is offering specialised training sessions on writing a dissertation.

43
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,300
Obviously this is not relevant to those of you who are undergraduates

44
00:03:06,300 --> 00:03:08,250
it is just for postgraduates.

45
00:03:08,250 --> 00:03:11,691
Your department will discuss the planning stage of the dissertation, i.e. what

46
00:03:11,691 --> 00:03:17,540
you're going to do with you, and we will focus on the structure of it.

47
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:21,800
However, the training will also include some time on the computers.

48
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,180
I realise most of you know how to organise files

49
00:03:25,180 --> 00:03:28,560
but we can show you the different ways to run data programmes.

50
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:33,508
Your tutors will tell you at the outset how to set out the chapters they require

51
00:03:33,508 --> 00:03:38,456
but you will need to ask them how they would like you to organise the bibliography

52
00:03:38,456 --> 00:03:40,930
because it varies depending on your subject area.

53
00:03:40,930 --> 00:03:47,050
When you've got something together the trainer here will look through the draft version for you to see if it's OK.

54
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:51,785
And, one final point, for those of you who have registered from abroad,

55
00:03:51,785 --> 00:03:56,520
we can offer individual sessions on dissertations if you feel you need them.

56
00:03:56,520 --> 00:03:58,620
If you require language lessons

57
00:03:58,720 --> 00:04:02,750
then they are available from the international Centre next to the Law Department.
